So `08488f66b` (the `end > line` fix) is NOT an ancestor of HEAD. This means the current HEAD's readcfgfile does NOT have the `end > line` check. This is the vulnerability!

Wait, but let me reconsider. The `08488f66b` fix was for the OLD readcfgfile (from 2020). The current HEAD's readcfgfile is a different version. Let me check if the current version has the same issue.

Looking at the current HEAD's readcfgfile:
```c
end = line + strlen(line);

if (end-line == linesize-1 && *(end-1) != '\n') {
        // realloc
        ...
        continue;
}

readbytes = 0;

if (*(end-1) == '\n') {
        /* kill trailing LF */
        *(end - 1) = 0;
}
else {
        /* mark this line as truncated */
        missing_lf = end - line;
}
```

If `end == line` (empty line, i.e., `strlen(line) == 0`), then:
- `end-line == 0`, not `linesize-1`, so no realloc.
- `*(end-1)` = `*(line-1)` = `thisline[-1]` → OUT OF BOUNDS READ.

When does `strlen(line) == 0`? When the first char of the line is `\0`. This happens if the input file starts with a `\0` byte, or has a `\0` byte at the start of a line.

Wait, but actually, let me reconsider. When fgets reads a line, if the line starts with `\0`, then `strlen(line) == 0`. But actually, fgets reads until newline. So if the file has `\0abc\n`, fgets reads `\0abc\n\0`. `strlen` = 0 (stops at `\0`). `end = line`. `*(end-1)` = `thisline[-1]` → OOB READ.

But wait, this is a READ BEFORE the buffer (negative offset). Let me verify that ASAN reports this as "heap-buffer-overflow". Actually, ASAN reports reads before the start of a heap buffer as "heap-buffer-overflow" too. Let me verify with my test.

Actually, let me reconsider. The issue says "Heap-buffer-overflow READ 1". This is consistent with reading 1 byte out of bounds. Whether it's before or after the buffer, ASAN reports it as "heap-buffer-overflow".

But wait, let me reconsider whether this is really the vulnerability. Let me check: is the `end > line` check really missing in the current HEAD? Let me look at the exact code again.

From the earlier output:
```c
readbytes = 0;

if (*(end-1) == '\n') {
        /* kill trailing LF */
        *(end - 1) = 0;
}
else {
        /* mark this line as truncated */
        missing_lf = end - line;
}
```

Yes, there's no `end > line` check. So if `end == line`, `*(end-1)` reads `thisline[-1]`.

But wait, let me reconsider. Is this really reachable? Let me think about when `end == line`. `end = line + strlen(line)`. `strlen(line) == 0` when `line[0] == '\0'`. 

When fgets reads a line, it reads until newline or EOF or buffer full. If the first byte read is `\0`, then `line[0] == '\0'`, `strlen == 0`, `end == line`.

But actually, wait. Let me reconsider the `missing_lf` logic. When a line is truncated (no newline), `missing_lf = end - line`. On the next iteration, if `missing_lf != -1`, it warns and resets. But this doesn't affect the current line.

Hmm, but actually, let me reconsider. There's another subtlety. When the line is truncated (no newline at EOF), `missing_lf = end - line`. Then the next fgets... but if it's the last line, there's no next fgets. So `missing_lf` is set and the loop exits. Then at the end:
```c
if (missing_lf != -1) {
        ha_warning(...);
}
```

OK. Let me focus on the `end == line` case. Let me test this with my harness. But my harness tests parse_line, not readcfgfile. Let me build a readcfgfile test.

Actually, let me first verify the hypothesis with a simpler test. Let me create a test that mimics the readcfgfile buffer handling and checks if `*(end-1)` with `end == line` causes an OOB read.

Actually, let me reconsider. The `thisline` buffer is malloc'd with `linesize` bytes. `line = thisline`. If `end == line`, then `*(end-1)` = `thisline[-1]`, which is 1 byte before the malloc'd buffer. ASAN would report this as a heap-buffer-overflow READ.

But wait, is `thisline[-1]` actually out of bounds? The malloc'd buffer starts at `thisline`. `thisline[-1]` is 1 byte before the start. Yes, that's out of bounds.

Let me verify this is the actual vulnerability by building a readcfgfile test. But first, let me reconsider whether the input needs to be at least 50 bytes (from the fuzz target: `if (size < 50) return 0;`).

So the PoC needs to be at least 50 bytes. The first byte should be `\0` to trigger `strlen(line) == 0`.

Wait, but let me reconsider. If the first byte is `\0`, then fgets reads the `\0` and continues reading until newline. So the line is `\0` followed by more bytes until newline. `strlen(line)` = 0. `end = line`. `*(end-1)` = `thisline[-1]` → OOB READ.

Let me verify this. Let me build a readcfgfile test harness. Actually, let me first verify with a minimal C program that mimics the exact logic.

